body:
@xfq I'm not familiar with the particular case folding issues, but my assumption is that input methods will apply the autocapitalization hint in a way that makes sense for each language. As @othermaciej noted for iOS, the way autocapitalization is currently implemented for a character (on both iOS and Android) is by making the shift key default to "on" for the next character to be typed, so I don't think we run into issues with changing the character to something the user isn't expecting.

I think if different languages should default to different autocapitalization behavior, that's something that the input method can do in response to the hint (e.g. having a language-specific behavior for the "default" state).


@annevk, @othermaciej I did notice that macOS Safari doesn't expose the IDL interface. I'm not sure how critical exposing the interface is for platforms that don't support the autocapitalize feature, but I suppose it would be good for cross-platform consistency? Chrome currently does expose the IDL the same way on all platforms, but there are cases where the IDL values are different from what Safari would provide (which I intend to fix once we agree on the desired behavior).


@r12a 

[1] I could add a note in the spec as to exactly how autocapitalization is currently implemented by on-screen keyboards (by toggling the sticky shift key). I think there are probably some other cases though like swipe input and voice input where it also makes sense to respect the attribute.

[2] I don't think there's really significant interaction with autocomplete here. I think that e.g. if sentence capitalization is enabled, and someone tries to start a sentence with "iPhone" but it comes out "IPhone" due to the autocapitalization, it's fine for autocomplete to correct that to "iPhone," despite the first letter no longer being capitalized. Autocomplete behavior is typically based on the language the current input method is configured with (for example, on Android, you can switch between different languages of Gboard)

[3], [4] This is an interesting idea; I'm not sure if demand has come up yet in practice though. The particular cases with Cherokee and Georgian seem better handled by the input method choosing to use only uppercase or lowercase letters as the default behavior and/or completely ignoring the autocapitalize attribute if appropriate.
